{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"BLOCKED BY RE-REVIEW, NOW REMEDIATED. The close-time plan-review re-run returned BLOCK with 5 blocking findings. They were correct and reduced to THREE distinct defects; all three are now fixed. Recording what changed, and — because it matters for how much the votes are worth — which of them the already-green gates had missed.\n\nDEFECT 1 (3 of the 5 blockers, one root cause): the story VIOLATED ITS OWN single-owner contract. The plan's Scope names `infra/terraform/iam_s7.tf` (new) as the deliverable and states `iam.tf` is NOT modified, citing iam.tf:4-9 and the established iam_s2.tf / iam_s4a.tf convention. The landed change instead added the policy directly to `iam.tf` (+47 lines), and no iam_s7.tf existed — so AC1 (\"`git diff --stat infra/terraform/iam.tf` shows no change\") was falsified by the as-landed code. FIXED: the block moved to a new `infra/terraform/iam_s7.tf` with a header matching the iam_s2/iam_s4a convention; `iam.tf` is now BYTE-IDENTICAL to its pre-S7 state (verified: `git diff HEAD~1 -- infra/terraform/iam.tf` is empty) and no longer appears in the commit at all. ZERO INFRASTRUCTURE IMPACT: both resource addresses (`data.aws_iam_policy_document.bedrock_converse`, `aws_iam_role_policy.bedrock_converse`) are unchanged and stay in the same module, so Terraform sees no diff — which is why the production verification already recorded for this story still holds and did not need redoing. `terraform fmt -check` clean.\n\nDEFECT 2: the PLAN text was stale against better code, in two places, one of them the story's central fact.\n  (a) The plan still asserted the grant is \"`bedrock:Converse` + `bedrock:ConverseStream` (NOT the `InvokeModel` pair)\" — the exact error this story's own code comment documents as \"got WRONG once\" and disproved from the instance. The landed code correctly grants InvokeModel + InvokeModelWithResponseStream. The plan has been rewritten to match, carrying the simulator-vs-service reasoning.\n  (b) The plan's inference-profile ARN was `arn:aws:bedrock:us-east-1:<acct>:inference-profile/us.anthropic.claude-*` — region-pinned AND `us.`-prefixed. The landed code is strictly better (`arn:aws:bedrock:*:<acct>:inference-profile/*.anthropic.claude-*`). The reviewer was right that an implementer following the plan literally would produce an under-scoped policy that denies `global.` profiles — and `global.` profiles are a MEASURED valid form in this account (896586841071/us-east-1), so that is a real runtime denial, not a hypothetical. Plan text now matches the code and says why both wildcards are load-bearing.\n\nDEFECT 3: `bedrock:InvokeModelWithResponseStream` was granted BY SYMMETRY and never exercised — which this story's own evidentiary standard (\"only a real call settles it\") forbids. Operator decision was to keep the grant and verify it for real rather than drop it. VERIFIED:\n\n  IDENTITY: arn:aws:sts::896586841071:assumed-role/rebar-gerrit-instance-role/i-00880b2c7f13527c5\n  boto3 bedrock-runtime converse_stream(us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6) -> STREAM_OK: ready\n\nRun from INSIDE the `compose-review-bot-1` container on the instance (the actual review-bot runtime), i.e. under the scoped instance role — NOT from the local operator identity, which is `user/joe_frontier` holding AdministratorAccess and therefore incapable of failing this test. Account and region confirmed as 896586841071 / us-east-1 before recording.\n\nTwo things learned doing it, both now in the runbook as section 3:\n  - The CLI CANNOT perform this check: aws-cli 2.33.15 on this AMI exposes NO streaming subcommand (`bedrock-runtime` offers only converse / invoke-model / the async trio). An unknown subcommand exits printing the CLI help stub, which reads like a failed call rather than a missing command — I hit exactly that and nearly misread it as a denial.\n  - SSM AWS-RunShellScript mangles the embedded JSON through shell quoting; base64-encoding the script is what made it reliable.\n\nSCOPE OF THAT PROOF, stated so it is not over-read: it shows the streaming path SUCCEEDS under this policy from the scoped role. It does NOT establish which IAM action ConverseStream authorizes against, because the policy grants both actions simultaneously; only removing one and re-testing would show that, and that deny-side control is S8's fault injection.\n\nGATE OBSERVATION worth carrying forward: none of these three were caught by the change's own `LLM-Review +1` or `Verified +1`. CI cannot see a plan/code contradiction, and the code review scored the diff on its merits without the plan's single-owner contract in view. The close-time plan-review re-run is what caught all three — because it is the only gate that reads the PLAN against the CODE. That is the same lesson the completion-verifier has now taught three times this run, from the other direction."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785443125498920001,"uuid":"4960cdb3-13ec-40b4-a720-0e5ba88e5e8e"}